From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 14:26:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] adb: a USB path that works on a kernel without functionfs AIO

This device's 3.10 kernel has no functionfs AIO, so adbd's default connection type
cannot work: it submits async reads that never complete and the daemon appears to
hang with the device stuck in "unauthorized".

Restores a blocking USB connection (UsbFfsBlockingConnection) and selects it when
the kernel cannot do AIO, and makes that path treat a zero-length bulk read as a
framing artifact rather than end-of-file. Both halves are needed: without the second,
the blocking path added by the first drops the connection the moment the host sends
a zero-length packet, which is exactly what it does between transfers.

The connection is a BlockingConnection behind BlockingConnectionAdapter, like the
TCP transport: reader and writer threads of its own, so a bulk write the host is
not draining blocks only the writer and never the fdevent loop (an earlier shape
blocked the caller of Connection::Write() and a host that stopped reading froze
adbd, TCP included). Close() signals each thread that is inside a syscall and
keeps signalling until it has left -- a single pthread_kill() can be eaten by the
no-op handler between the closed check and the read(), which left the reader
blocked and the destructor's join() hung. The handler goes in via sigaction()
without SA_RESTART (bionic's signal() sets it, and the read that waits for
FUNCTIONFS_ENABLE returns -ERESTARTSYS), and the I/O is
unix_read_interruptible()/adb_writev(), because adb_read()/adb_write() are
TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY and re-issue the call on EINTR themselves. This kernel's f_fs
fails every endpoint read/write with ENODEV after a DISABLE until the files are
re-opened, so the transport is torn down on the first failed read and
usb_ffs_open_thread opens a fresh one for the next ENABLE.

Was two patches, the second fixing code the first introduced in the same file.
---
 daemon/usb.cpp | 253 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 250 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/daemon/usb.cpp b/daemon/usb.cpp
index 6c3f735e..18e135f3 100644
--- a/daemon/usb.cpp
+++ b/daemon/usb.cpp
@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@
 
 #include <errno.h>
 #include <inttypes.h>
+#include <signal.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <stdlib.h>
 #include <string.h>
@@ -32,10 +33,12 @@
 
 #include <algorithm>
 #include <array>
+#include <atomic>
 #include <future>
 #include <memory>
 #include <mutex>
 #include <optional>
+#include <thread>
 #include <vector>
 
 #include <asyncio/AsyncIO.h>
@@ -51,6 +54,7 @@
 #include "daemon/property_monitor.h"
 #include "daemon/usb_ffs.h"
 #include "sysdeps/chrono.h"
+#include "sysdeps/uio.h"
 #include "transfer_id.h"
 #include "transport.h"
 #include "types.h"
@@ -726,6 +730,240 @@ struct UsbFfsConnection : public Connection {
     static constexpr int kInterruptionSignal = SIGUSR1;
 };
 
+
+// Blocking FunctionFS connection, for kernels whose functionfs predates AIO.
+//
+// UsbFfsConnection above drives the bulk endpoints with io_submit(). FFS only
+// grew .aio_read/.aio_write in the 3.15 era; on an older kernel
+// ffs_epfile_operations has just .read/.write, so io_submit() returns EINVAL
+// and the transport is destroyed the instant it enumerates:
+//
+//   UsbFfs: connection terminated: failed to submit read: Invalid argument
+//
+// The host then sees a device that appears, reports "offline", and never
+// answers CNXN -- indistinguishable from an authorisation problem, but nothing
+// to do with keys. AOSP used to ship this fallback as usb_legacy.cpp, selected
+// by sys.usb.ffs.aio_compat, and dropped it when adb became a mainline module.
+// This restores the same idea under the same property name.
+//
+// It is a BlockingConnection behind BlockingConnectionAdapter, like the TCP
+// transport: the adapter owns a reader thread and a writer thread, so a bulk
+// write that the host is not draining blocks only the writer, never the fdevent
+// loop that calls Connection::Write() (an earlier version of this class blocked
+// the caller, and a host that stopped reading froze adbd -- TCP included).
+//
+// A blocking endpoint read()/write() is only interruptible by a signal. Close()
+// therefore signals the threads that are inside a syscall, and keeps signalling
+// until each one reports it has left: a single pthread_kill() can land between
+// the "are we closed" check and the read() and is then eaten by the no-op
+// handler, which is a reader that never exits and a destructor join() that never
+// returns -- adbd was wedged that way after the first USB re-enumeration.
+// The syscalls are unix_read_interruptible()/adb_writev(): adb_read()/adb_write()
+// are TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY and re-issue the call on EINTR, and bionic's signal()
+// would have installed the handler with SA_RESTART, either of which hides the
+// interruption from this code.
+//
+// FUNCTIONFS_DISABLE needs no event monitor here: the reader is always inside a
+// read(), this kernel's f_fs fails that read (ESHUTDOWN), marks the endpoint
+// files errored so every later read()/write() on them fails with ENODEV until
+// they are re-opened, and the adapter tears the transport down;
+// usb_ffs_open_thread then re-opens ep0 and the endpoints and a fresh connection
+// comes up on the next ENABLE. ep0 is still drained on its own thread so the
+// event queue cannot back up and UNBIND is logged, but nothing depends on it.
+struct UsbFfsBlockingConnection : public BlockingConnection {
+    UsbFfsBlockingConnection(unique_fd control, unique_fd read, unique_fd write,
+                             std::promise<void> destruction_notifier)
+        : destruction_notifier_(std::move(destruction_notifier)),
+          control_fd_(std::move(control)),
+          read_fd_(std::move(read)),
+          write_fd_(std::move(write)) {
+        LOG(INFO) << "UsbFfsBlockingConnection constructed (no-AIO fallback)";
+        static std::once_flag handler_once;
+        std::call_once(handler_once, []() {
+            // Not signal(): bionic's signal() sets SA_RESTART, and the endpoint read that
+            // waits for FUNCTIONFS_ENABLE returns -ERESTARTSYS, so the kernel would restart
+            // it transparently and the interrupt would never surface as EINTR.
+            struct sigaction sa = {};
+            sa.sa_handler = [](int) {};
+            sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
+            sa.sa_flags = 0;
+            sigaction(kInterruptionSignal, &sa, nullptr);
+        });
+        StartEp0Monitor();
+    }
+
+    ~UsbFfsBlockingConnection() override {
+        LOG(INFO) << "UsbFfsBlockingConnection being destroyed";
+        Close();
+        if (monitor_thread_.joinable()) monitor_thread_.join();
+        control_fd_.reset();
+        read_fd_.reset();
+        write_fd_.reset();
+        destruction_notifier_.set_value();
+    }
+
+    // Called on the adapter's read thread.
+    bool Read(apacket* packet) override final {
+        if (!ReadFully(reader_, &packet->msg, sizeof(packet->msg))) return false;
+        if (packet->msg.data_length > MAX_PAYLOAD) {
+            LOG(ERROR) << "USB read: payload too large: " << packet->msg.data_length;
+            return false;
+        }
+        packet->payload.resize(packet->msg.data_length);
+        if (packet->msg.data_length &&
+            !ReadFully(reader_, packet->payload.data(), packet->payload.size())) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "USB read: " << dump_header(&packet->msg);
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    // Called on the adapter's write thread.
+    bool Write(apacket* packet) override final {
+        LOG(DEBUG) << "USB write: " << dump_header(&packet->msg);
+        if (!WriteFully(writer_, &packet->msg, sizeof(packet->msg))) return false;
+        if (!packet->payload.empty() &&
+            !WriteFully(writer_, packet->payload.data(), packet->payload.size())) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    bool DoTlsHandshake(RSA* key, std::string* auth_key) override final {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "TLS handshake is not supported on the blocking USB path";
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    // Must make concurrent Read()/Write() return; the adapter joins its threads right after.
+    void Close() override final {
+        if (closed_.exchange(true)) return;
+        LOG(INFO) << "UsbFfsBlockingConnection closing";
+        Interrupt(reader_);
+        Interrupt(writer_);
+        Interrupt(monitor_);
+    }
+
+    void Reset() override final { Close(); }
+
+  private:
+    // Matches USB_FFS_BULK_SIZE from the pre-AIO adbd. Reads are chunked because
+    // a single bulk transfer is bounded and a payload may span several.
+    static constexpr size_t kBulkChunk = 16384;
+    static constexpr int kInterruptionSignal = SIGUSR1;
+
+    // One per thread that can block in a syscall on our fds. in_syscall_ is set
+    // around the call so Close() can tell whether a signal is still needed.
+    struct Blocker {
+        std::atomic<bool> in_syscall{false};
+        std::atomic<pthread_t> tid{};
+    };
+
+    // Enter/leave a possibly-blocking syscall. Returns false if Close() already ran,
+    // so the caller bails without touching the fd.
+    bool Enter(Blocker& b) {
+        b.tid.store(pthread_self());
+        b.in_syscall.store(true);
+        if (closed_.load()) {
+            b.in_syscall.store(false);
+            return false;
+        }
+        return true;
+    }
+    void Leave(Blocker& b) { b.in_syscall.store(false); }
+
+    // Signal until the thread has left its syscall. Bounded only so a kernel that
+    // will not wake a sleeper (it should) produces a log line instead of a silent hang.
+    void Interrupt(Blocker& b) {
+        for (int i = 0; b.in_syscall.load(); ++i) {
+            pthread_kill(b.tid.load(), kInterruptionSignal);
+            if (i == 2000) {  // ~10s
+                LOG(ERROR) << "UsbFfsBlockingConnection: thread ignored " << i << " interrupts";
+            }
+            std::this_thread::sleep_for(5ms);
+        }
+    }
+
+    bool WriteFully(Blocker& b, const void* data, size_t len) {
+        const char* p = static_cast<const char*>(data);
+        while (len > 0) {
+            if (!Enter(b)) return false;
+            // adb_writev() is a bare writev(); adb_write() is TEMP_FAILURE_RETRY and
+            // would swallow the EINTR.
+            adb_iovec iov = {const_cast<char*>(p), len};
+            ssize_t rc = adb_writev(write_fd_, &iov, 1);
+            Leave(b);
+            if (rc < 0 && errno == EINTR) continue;  // Enter() returns false once closed
+            if (rc <= 0) {
+                if (!closed_) PLOG(ERROR) << "blocking USB write failed";
+                return false;
+            }
+            p += rc;
+            len -= rc;
+        }
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    bool ReadFully(Blocker& b, void* data, size_t len) {
+        char* p = static_cast<char*>(data);
+        // A bulk transfer whose length is an exact multiple of the endpoint's max
+        // packet size is terminated by a zero-length packet, which surfaces here as
+        // a 0-byte read. That is a framing marker, not an error or EOF: treating it
+        // as failure tore the transport down mid-transfer, which is why a large
+        // host->device push died while device->host pulls looked fine.
+        int zero_reads = 0;
+        while (len > 0) {
+            size_t want = std::min(kBulkChunk, len);
+            if (!Enter(b)) return false;
+            ssize_t rc = unix_read_interruptible(read_fd_, p, want);
+            Leave(b);
+            if (rc < 0 && errno == EINTR) continue;
+            if (rc == 0) {
+                // Guard against a genuinely dead endpoint spinning here forever.
+                if (++zero_reads > 64) {
+                    if (!closed_) LOG(ERROR) << "blocking USB read: endpoint stuck at EOF";
+                    return false;
+                }
+                continue;
+            }
+            if (rc < 0) {
+                if (!closed_) PLOG(ERROR) << "blocking USB read failed";
+                return false;
+            }
+            zero_reads = 0;
+            p += rc;
+            len -= rc;
+        }
+        return true;
+    }
+
+    void StartEp0Monitor() {
+        monitor_thread_ = std::thread([this]() {
+            adb_thread_setname("UsbFfs-blk-ep0");
+            while (!closed_) {
+                struct usb_functionfs_event event;
+                if (!Enter(monitor_)) break;
+                ssize_t rc = unix_read_interruptible(control_fd_, &event, sizeof(event));
+                Leave(monitor_);
+                if (rc < 0 && errno == EINTR) continue;
+                if (rc != sizeof(event)) {
+                    if (!closed_) PLOG(WARNING) << "ep0 read failed";
+                    break;
+                }
+                LOG(INFO) << "USB event: "
+                          << to_string(static_cast<usb_functionfs_event_type>(event.type));
+            }
+        });
+    }
+
+    std::atomic<bool> closed_{false};
+    Blocker reader_, writer_, monitor_;
+    std::thread monitor_thread_;
+    std::promise<void> destruction_notifier_;
+    unique_fd control_fd_;
+    unique_fd read_fd_;
+    unique_fd write_fd_;
+};
+
 static void usb_ffs_open_thread() {
     adb_thread_setname("usb ffs open");
 
@@ -762,9 +1000,18 @@ static void usb_ffs_open_thread() {
         transport->serial = "UsbFfs";
         std::promise<void> destruction_notifier;
         std::future<void> future = destruction_notifier.get_future();
-        transport->SetConnection(std::make_unique<UsbFfsConnection>(
-                std::move(control), std::move(bulk_out), std::move(bulk_in),
-                std::move(destruction_notifier)));
+        // Kernels without FunctionFS AIO cannot service io_submit(); fall back to
+        // blocking reads and writes. Same property name AOSP's usb_legacy.cpp used.
+        if (android::base::GetBoolProperty("sys.usb.ffs.aio_compat", false)) {
+            transport->SetConnection(std::make_unique<BlockingConnectionAdapter>(
+                    std::make_unique<UsbFfsBlockingConnection>(
+                            std::move(control), std::move(bulk_out), std::move(bulk_in),
+                            std::move(destruction_notifier))));
+        } else {
+            transport->SetConnection(std::make_unique<UsbFfsConnection>(
+                    std::move(control), std::move(bulk_out), std::move(bulk_in),
+                    std::move(destruction_notifier)));
+        }
         register_transport(transport);
         future.wait();
     }
